title: Attribution
pid: 3ab20699-6feb-4200-966e-c9f8406a13f0
body: |2-
  A reply is often made by forking someone's note and writing under the
  fork. Read later, the fork would look like the forker's own words. So
  the fork operation records, in the clone's file, where the clone came
  from: its *attribution*. Its herald then reads
  =⌂:<FORKER/ORIGINAL>=, the labels of the clone's home skgrepo and of
  the skgrepo it was forked from, wherever the clone is drawn, even
  under a note of its own skgrepo, where no other node shows its home
  skgrepo, unless the note above it says the same. A note of its home
  drawn under it, with no attribution of the same skgrepo, says its own
  =⌂:FORKER=, so the herald marks where the voice switches back
  ('docs/heralds.org', "Other heralds"). A fork of Claude's note into Jeff's skgrepo =jeff-decid=
  reads =⌂:<jeff-decid/claude-public>=.
